Q: How can I stop seeing JVM Full Thread dumps in my AWS EMR Spark job stdout logs? A: <p>I have a solution for viewing the logs on the the instance itself or other Unix environment.</p>
<p>By piping the output through a filter we can remove the stacktrace when reading or tailing the logs.</p>
<p><strong>Update 2026:</strong><br />
I now use:</p>
<pre><code>less_log() {
  # Pattern 1: The start of the dump
  # Pattern 2: A timestamp (4 digits for year or a bracket)
  awk '
    /^Full thread dump/ { in_block=1; next }
    /^[0-9]{4}-|^\[[0-9]/ { in_block=0 }
    !in_block { print $0; fflush() }
  ' &quot;$1&quot; | less
}
</code></pre>
<p>and</p>
<pre><code>tail_log() {
  tail -f &quot;$1&quot; | awk '/^Full thread dump/ {ib=1; next} /^[0-9]{4}-|^\[[0-9]/ {ib=0} !ib {print $0; fflush()}' | less
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Original Answer</strong></p>
<p>The filter removes all lines between <code>^Full thread dump</code> and <code>^\[[0-9</code>
Which works for me because my logs start with <code>[2023-09-7 ...</code>
This has been tested in both bash and zsh</p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>less_log() { 
  START_PAT=&quot;^Full thread dump&quot;
  END_PAT=&quot;^\[[0-9]&quot;

  IN_BLOCK=0;
  tail -f -n +1 $1 | while read LINE; do 
    if [[ $IN_BLOCK -eq 1 ]]; then
      if [[ &quot;$LINE&quot; =~ $END_PAT ]]; then
        IN_BLOCK=0;
        echo $LINE;
      fi
    elif [[ &quot;$LINE&quot; =~ $START_PAT ]]; then
      IN_BLOCK=1;
    else
      echo $LINE;
    fi 
  done | less
}
</code></pre>
<p>You can now view the logs like this</p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>less_log /var/log/hadoop-yarn/containers/application_1693901863825_0025/container_1693901863825_0025_01_000001/stdout
</code></pre>
